WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.129
Since the release of 2008's Iron Man, multiple
writers, tech experts, and financial wizards

00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:09.280
have worked hard to find out just how much
money Tony Stark spent being Iron Man.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.120
Some of these numbers are surprisingly low
— and some of them are straight-up astronomical

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.460
— but one thing's for certain: Being the
Iron Avenger ain't cheap.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.820
In 2010 — the same year Iron Man 2 was released
— io9's Annalee Newitz crunched the numbers

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.420
and learned that the total for all the parts
comprising Iron Man's suit came to around

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.620
$100 million.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.080
Newitz pointed out that this was around $12
million less than the cost of an F-35 fighter

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:31.580
plane.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.140
You probably wouldn't find them in a big box
store, but hypothetically it wouldn't be out

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.800
of the reach of the U.S. military.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.309
The story is a decade old, so why aren't there
already fleets of Iron Man suits?

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:43.320
Well, probably because of a couple of the
story's missing pieces.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.160
First, Newitz comes to the figure with only
five components of the hypothetical suit — a

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.219
helmet-mounted display, an exoskeleton, a
portable nuclear power source, a jet pack,

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:53.510
and wearable computers.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.120
No weapons are mentioned, which are crucial
to being Iron Man.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.040
There are many other missing suit capabilities,
like Tony's, uh… waste filtration system.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.380
"You know, the question I get asked most often
is: 'Tony, how do you go to the bathroom in

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.000
the suit?'

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:09.900
...Just like that."

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.200
Second, even if it were a complete list, the
story is missing a critical component: the

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.060
research and development necessary to make
all these different pieces of tech work together

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:18.840
in one suit.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.400
That kind of brain power comes with a hefty
— and unknowable — price tag.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.580
A couple of months after the 2012 release
of Avengers, MoneySupermarket.com gave the

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:30.860
cost of being Iron Man a much steeper sum
than io9's quote.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.100
The site estimated the cost of living as both
Tony Stark and Iron Man would be well over

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.400
15 times greater, putting it at $1.6 billion.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.960
Unlike the 2010 story, MoneySupermarket.com's
data took into account things like the anti-personnel

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.920
guns on the suit's shoulders — $400,000
each — the rocket launchers in the wrists,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.060
which would clock in at $1.5 million; and
the development of the artificial intelligence

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.760
program known as JARVIS at around $10 million.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.560
MoneySupermarket gauged the total cost for
all of Tony Stark's suits combined by the

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.220
end of The Avengers at $1.5 billion.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.250
The site also went beyond the suit and looked
at Tony's lifestyle.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:09.080
They put his mansion at $25 million and his
fancy car collection at $3.4 million.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.660
So while most of that $1.6 billion goes toward
the suits, it's good to know he gives himself

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.720
a modest amount of cash to just have a little
fun.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.700
A little less than a year after MoneySupermarket.com
released its data putting the cost of being

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.440
Iron Man at $1.6 billion, the same site released
a new estimate of $10 billion.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:31.220
By 2013, Stark's collection of suits increases
from seven to 42, adding $7 billion to this

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.840
estimate — and, honestly, that's probably
a little low.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.939
Their data only counts seven of Tony's 35
new suits.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.299
Iron Man 3's final battle and Tony's "House
Party" protocol clearly shows way more than

00:02:41.299 --> 00:02:46.480
seven new suits, so if seven suits cost $7
billion, you can likely add around $30 billion

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.080
to the total.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.360
And whatever that total is?

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.810
Well, that's what Tony throws away on a romantic
gesture to Pepper by ordering the surviving

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.700
suits to self-destruct…only to have new
suits ready to go by Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.520
There's one big-ticket item just about everyone
forgets in their assessment of Tony's spending

00:03:01.529 --> 00:03:04.000
— Veronica, a.k.a. the Hulkbuster suit.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.219
"The whole team's down.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:06.580
We've got no backup here."

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.840
"I'm calling in Veronica."

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.900
In particular, there's the method of the Hulkbuster
suit's delivery.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.319
When Tony activates Veronica in 2015's Avengers:
Age of Ultron, we learn he isn't keeping the

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.160
armor in Stark Tower or a compound in upstate
New York.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.380
The Hulkbuster armor waits in orbit to be
deployed by a satellite.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.240
That means that along with developing and
building the suit, Veronica needed to be attached

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.620
to a satellite which needed to be launched
into orbit.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.280
So while we can't tell you how much it would
cost Tony to build the Hulkbuster armor, we

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.300
do know launching a satellite would cost him
between $50 million and $400 million all on

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:42.800
its own.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.080
While we don't work for NASA, we're going
to guess that — considering that satellite

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.569
needs the added capability of launching Veronica
back to Earth at a moment's notice — Veronica's

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:52.680
costs would be closer to the high end of that
estimate.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.700
In 2017 — the same year Tony acted as Peter
Parker's father figure in Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:01.150
— the billionaire got some good news from
a new estimate of his spending needs.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.430
According to a video made by Coinage, Time
Inc.'s personal finance video company, being

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:11.930
Iron Man would cost $8.4 billion — a $1.6
billion drop from MoneySupermarket.com's 2013

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.239
estimate of $10 billion.

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:17.560
Coinage's explanation isn't nearly as comprehensive
as far breaking down exactly how they arrived

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.380
at that number, so it's curious how that $1.6
billion went missing.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.860
We'll leave someone with Tony Stark-level
intelligence to sus that one out — but the

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.740
most likely reason for this discrepancy?

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.620
Someone hasn't been keeping up with the MCU
movies.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.280
Shame, shame…

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.480
"It's, it's… it's embarrassing."

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.060
As comprehensive as any of the analyses of
Tony Stark's costs may be, there's a lot missing.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.949
No one knows, for example, the cost of the
Spider-Man suits Tony makes, the Hulkbuster

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:45.569
suit, most of the 42 suits he'd made by Iron
Man 3, or the automated arms used to peel

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:46.340
the armor off.

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.440
We don't know what Age of Ultron's Iron Legion
cost, the new nano-fiber suit Tony debuts

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.780
in Infinity War, or the time machine in Endgame.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.240
Then there are personnel costs.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.800
Tony has to pay for all the food and other
amenities provided for the Avengers at their

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.340
compound, not to mention that he presumably
pays them salaries.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:05.540
He's got Quinjets and helicopters, he's got
multiple satellites, he buys a giant stuffed

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.840
bunny for Pepper in Iron Man 3, and he also
has to pay for that dude's phone he smashes

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.280
after he threatens the Mandarin.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:13.860
"That's what you wanted, right?"

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:17.080
"Bill me."

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.650
Then there's maintenance.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.199
You think Tony can build one of these insanely
advanced Iron Man suits and not bother with

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:23.700
tune-ups?

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:28.160
Every high-tech thing Tony invents is an ongoing
investment, so it's arguably fortunate they

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.740
usually get destroyed after only a couple
of uses.

